## 2. Task Table  

| Name | Text | Relation | Location | Number of Robots | Justification |
|------|------|----------|----------|------------------|---------------|
| **AT1** | Robot queries the hospital database for patient’s ability to fetch food and presence of companion or nurse. | AND | hospital (database) | 1 | Only one robot is needed to perform the query; the data is stored centrally. |
| **AT2** | Robot places the tray in the patient’s reach and waits for patient/companion to retrieve the meal. | AND | inpatient room | 1 | The robot handles the tray; human cooperation is outside the robot’s control. |
| **AT3** | Robot manipulates food onto the patient’s table using its special manipulation skill. | AND | inpatient room | 1 | The robot alone can perform the delicate table‑delivery maneuver. |
| **AT4** | Robot announces which meal patient should retrieve (e.g., “Please take your salad”). | AND | inpatient room | 1 | Direct communication from the robot to the patient. |
| **AT5** | Robot alerts staff if the patient retrieves the wrong meal (e.g., by sounding an alarm or notifying the nurse). | AND | inpatient room | 1 | The robot can immediately trigger the alert once the wrong meal is detected. |
| **AT6** | Robot collects dirty dishes from the room, possibly opening the door if it is closed. | AND | inpatient room | `[1,2]` | Dish retrieval can be done by a single robot (unassisted) or by two cooperating robots; the robot may need to open the door. |
| **AT7** | Robot opens the room door, possibly with human assistance. | AND | room door | 1 | The robot can handle the door; human cooperation may be required when the door is locked. |

